Formalizing and validating the GC rooting discipline
=====================================================

STATUS: design note / discussion record — NOT yet an actionable work item.
This records a discussion about how to eliminate (or at least systematically
validate) the recurring use-after-free bug class described in the memory note
"bocl GC rooting" and in issues/closed/live-pool-reroot-audit.issue.


1. The bug class
----------------

bocl uses a PRECISE, EXPLICITLY-ROOTED, NON-MOVING collector.  Roots are the
live pool (sources/memory.c, open/add/close_live_pool) plus globals
(obarray / packages).  The C stack is NOT scanned.  Therefore a Ref held only
in a C local — not in the live pool and not reachable from a rooted object —
is invisible to mark() and is freed by any intervening GC.  A later use is a
use-after-free.

Two facts make this subtler than "is it in a Rooted<T>?":

  (a) allocate() auto-roots its own result (add_to_live_pool) and zeroes the
      body, so a fresh allocation IS rooted and half-built objects are safe.

  (b) Object_new and the *_slots constructors open_live_pool()...close_live_pool(),
      which TRIMS the result back out (LIFO).  So the caller must
      add_to_live_pool() the result before the next allocation.

The live pool is therefore exactly a HandleScope / RAII rooting scope:
close_live_pool == leaving the scope; a missing add_to_live_pool in the caller
== a missing scope.Escape(handle).  Rootedness is LIFO-scoped, not monotonic.

The property to guarantee:  at every safepoint (any point that may GC — in a
Lisp, nearly every call), every Ref that is live (used later) must be rooted.


2. The ~800 open_live_pool sites are NOT a per-type count
---------------------------------------------------------

A count of ~800 open_live_pool calls across the tree does NOT reflect ~800 data
types.  open_live_pool is not the allocation primitive (allocate() is); it is a
per-FUNCTION rooting scope.  The 800 tracks "functions that hold live Refs
across a possible-GC point", not types.  Three populations:

  - Built-in wrappers (built_in_functions.c ~197): uniform shape —
    open; root function+arguments; pop args; call the real C function;
    store result; close.  Pure boilerplate, ~nil bug risk.

  - Struct ctors/accessors (kernel_structure_functions.c ~77,
    structure_functions.c ~94): uniform — root params; make_structure_instance;
    structure_set per slot; close.  Derivable from the defstruct.

  - Genuine constructors + ad-hoc helpers (Object_new, cons_new, *_slots,
    environment_new, map_walk, Closure_slots, ...): a few dozen, varied,
    hand-rolled.  THIS is where every historical bug lived (see the memory
    note's fix list: environment_new callers, map_walk accumulator,
    Closure_slots args).

So ~370 of the 800 are mechanical boilerplate that never contained a bug.  This
is a factorization smell, and reducing it is itself a verification strategy:

  - Macro-collapse the scope into WITH_LIVE_POOL { ... } using
    __attribute__((cleanup)) on a scope guard.  This deletes ~800 hand-written
    close_live_pool calls AND closes a latent bug: any early return / FATAL /
    condition-signal (longjmp) BETWEEN open and close skips the close and
    mismatches the pool.  (FATAL already appears between open and close in
    kernel_structure_functions.c.)  A cleanup macro makes the bracket
    non-local-exit safe and un-forgettable.

  - GENERATE the uniform wrappers.  bocl already generates kernel_functions.h;
    generating the ~370 wrapper/accessor BODIES from a declarative arglist/slot
    spec removes them from the hand-audited set (correct-by-construction) and
    replaces "verify 370 sites" with "verify the generator once".

  - The residue — a few dozen genuine constructors — is the real verification
    target, and small enough that formal methods become tractable.


3. Layered validation plan (for the residue)
---------------------------------------------

Full Coq/ACL2 proof of every call site against a C semantics is NOT the right
first target (hundreds of sites, call graph obscured by generic dispatch
evale/apply/CLOS, moving target).  Layered instead:

  A. ACL2 executable model + invariant (do first; we are a Lisp shop, the GC
     *is* Lisp).  Model heap/roots/live-pool/mark/sweep/minor+remset as ACL2
     functions mirroring memory.c.  Prove the core safety invariant:
       forall a in reachable(roots,heap). heap_after_gc(a) = heap_before_gc(a)
     (full and minor, with the remset theorem for old->young).  Then prove a
     caller-hazard theorem over a tiny imperative IL: a trace with no static
     hazard never dereferences a freed address.  DIFFERENTIAL ORACLE: dump the
     allocation/GC trace from the existing BOCL_GCSTRESS/BOCL_POISON harness,
     replay through the ACL2 model, assert heap/root state matches the C.

  B. Static hazard analysis (the workhorse; catches regressions in CI).  A
     Clang/LLVM intra-procedural dataflow modeled on SpiderMonkey's rootAnalysis.
     Lattice per Ref-local: {Rooted, Unrooted, Maybe} tagged by live-pool DEPTH
     (LIFO), so close_live_pool(p) demotes everything rooted only below p.
     Transfer: allocate -> Rooted; trimming ctor -> Unrooted; add_to_live_pool
     -> Rooted; store into rooted slot -> Rooted; close_live_pool -> demote;
     call args -> Rooted-by-contract.  Hazard = Unrooted AND live-after at a
     may-GC point.  Treat every call conservatively as a safepoint (matches the
     Lisp reality: almost everything may GC).  VALIDATE against the 72-site
     ground-truth in issues/closed/live-pool-reroot-audit.issue to measure
     false-positive/negative rates before rolling out.

  C. (Optional, highest assurance) VST/CompCert proof of the ACTUAL C of
     allocate/mark/sweep/add/close_live_pool + 2-3 canonical constructors
     against the rooting spec.  Core only, not all callers.

  Phase 0 (shared bridge): write the rooting CONTRACT as machine-readable
  annotations per function — may-GC (default yes) and result-rooting-effect
  (Rooted like allocate vs Trimmed like Object_new/*_slots).  The 72-site audit
  is the validation corpus.

Recommendation: do B and A together; skip/defer C.  But first do the
refactor in section 2 — the safest code is the code you no longer hand-write.


4. Conservative stack scanning — CONSIDERED AND REJECTED (for now)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

The one architectural lever that collapses the rooting OBLIGATION to ~zero
(no function need root anything) is conservative stack + register scanning
(Boehm model).  It is attractive here because bocl's collector is NON-MOVING:
conservative roots need no pinning, cannot corrupt (valid_block_start_p already
guards false pointers), cost is only some floating garbage.  It would delete the
entire bug class rather than validate it.

DECISION (2026-07-19): avoid it, on PORTABILITY grounds.  Target is any
C17 + POSIX platform.  C gives no portable handle on the stack:

  - stacks grow up on some ISAs/ABIs, down on others (addressing-wise);
  - locating stack base/extent is non-portable (no C17/POSIX API — needs
    pthread_attr_getstack, /proc, __libc_stack_end, mach APIs, TIB walks...
    all platform-specific and each with caveats);
  - flushing register windows / callee-saved registers to the stack is
    ISA-specific (SPARC register windows, setjmp-does-not-guarantee-all-regs);
  - split/segmented/discontiguous stacks and green threads break the
    "one contiguous range" assumption entirely.

So conservative scanning trades the rooting-discipline problem for a
platform-portability problem, against our C17+POSIX-portability goal.  Needs
more reflection before it could be reconsidered (e.g. as an OPTIONAL backend on
platforms where the stack can be located reliably, with precise rooting as the
portable fallback).


5. CLISP-style Lisp stack — and what we ALREADY have (2026-07-19)
----------------------------------------------------------------

CLISP uses two stacks: a Lisp stack (STACK) holding every live Lisp reference,
and the C stack (control flow only, no refs).  The GC scans STACK precisely;
portable, no C-stack scanning.  Question raised: don't we already have a Lisp
stack via the bytecode VM?

FINDING: YES, for compiled code.  The VM's operand stack and locals are NOT C
arrays — make_vm_vector() (bytecode.c:169) allocates a real VECTOR_Class heap
object, and each activation roots both via add_to_live_pool (bytecode.c:349).
So every Ref a compiled function pushes on S[sp] lives inside a rooted,
precisely-traced vector; the interpreter loop needs no per-value rooting.  The
CLISP property already holds inside the VM.

BUT that does NOT retire the rooting bug class, because the bugs are not in the
VM's operand stack — they are in the C-coded kernel primitives/constructors the
VM (and the tree-walker) CALL OUT to (Object_new, *_slots, environment_new,
map_walk...), which hold Refs in C locals across allocations.  A Lisp stack
traces the caller's values; it does nothing for a callee C function's locals.
CLISP closes that only because its primitives keep working refs ON STACK
(pushSTACK/popSTACK) instead of in C locals — that discipline, not merely
"having a second stack", is the fix.

DEEPER FRAMING: bocl ALREADY HAS an explicit Lisp reference stack — the live
pool itself is one (open/add/close = scope/push/pop, GC-scanned, portable, no
C-stack scan).  The difference from CLISP is SHADOW-stack vs PRIMARY-stack:
  - live pool = SHADOW stack: the value lives in a C local Ref x;
    add_to_live_pool(x) copies the pointer in as a SECOND root.  Bugs come from
    the duplication — forget the shadow, or the shadow is trimmed while the C
    local still holds the now-dangling value.
  - CLISP STACK = PRIMARY stack: the value lives ONLY on the stack; placement IS
    existence IS rootedness.  A live-but-unrooted value is not representable.
So CLISP's advantage is not the Lisp stack (we have one) but that its primitives
never hold a live Ref in a C local.  Adopting it = rewriting the residue of
genuine constructors to pushSTACK/popSTACK (same portability, one contiguous
scan vs the segmented live-pool block list, arguably cleaner) — the same
few-dozen-site rewrite as section 2, but the PRINCIPLED version that makes the
bug unrepresentable rather than merely easier to detect.

Synthesis: (1) the VM already protects compiled code, so routing more execution
through the VM shrinks the C-local surface naturally; (2) for the residue, the
primary-stack idiom IS the principled form of the WITH_LIVE_POOL / codegen fix
in section 2 — prefer it when touching those sites.


6. Open questions / next steps
------------------------------

- Reconsider whether the per-function rooting OBLIGATION can be reduced without
  stack scanning (e.g. a shadow-stack macro that auto-registers locals, keeping
  precision + portability but minimizing boilerplate).
- Prototype the WITH_LIVE_POOL cleanup macro and the non-local-exit safety it
  buys; audit for open/close pairs currently straddling FATAL/signal.
- Assess generating the ~370 uniform wrapper/accessor bodies.
- Prototype Phase-B rootedness dataflow against the 72-site audit corpus to
  measure how many real bugs a static checker would have caught before
  investing in the ACL2 proof.

